\title{Equality types}
\import{prelude}

\p{Besides using a \code{\kwi{Prop}} asserted as a field, the other method of forming a proposition is \defcase{equality}. Given two elements \code{a0} and \code{a1} of \em{any} set \code{A : \kwi{Set}}, there is a proposition \code{(a0 = a1) : \kwi{Prop}} which is true (i.e. has an element) exactly when \code{a0} is equal to \code{a1}.}

\p{We can use this to write down laws in our theories that involve equality. For instance:}

\pre{%
\kw{theory} CharliesBar := \kw{sig}
  ChocolateBar : \kwi{Set}
  Contents : \kwi{Set}
  unwrap : ChocolateBar -> Contents
  charlies-bar : ChocolateBar
  golden-ticket : Contents
  plot-armour : unwrap charlies-bar = golden-ticket
\kw{end}
}

\dbexample{
  \p{\strong{Table \code{root.ChocolateBar}}}

  \table{
    \row{\hcell{Row ID}}
  }{
    \row{\cell{\code{b1}}}
    \row{\cell{\code{b2}}}
  }

  \p{\strong{Table \code{root.Contents}}}

  \table{
    \row{\hcell{Row ID}}
  }{
    \row{\cell{\code{c1}}}
    \row{\cell{\code{c2}}}
  }

  \p{\strong{Table \code{root.unwrap}}}

  \table{
    \row{\hcell{\code{a : root.ChocolateBar}} \hcell{\code{b : root.Contents}}}
  }{
    \row{\cell{\code{b1}} \cell{\code{c1}}}
    \row{\cell{\code{b2}} \cell{\code{c2}}}
  }

  \p{\strong{Table \code{root.charlies-bar}}}

  \table{
    \row{\hcell{\code{a : root.ChocolateBar}}}
  }{
    \row{\cell{\code{b1}}}
  }

  \p{\strong{Table \code{root.golden-ticket}}}

  \table{
    \row{\hcell{\code{a : root.Contents}}}
  }{
    \row{\cell{\code{c1}}}
  }

  \p{\strong{Constraints:}}
  \ul{
    \li{Each value in columns \code{a} and \code{b} of \code{root.unwrap} is a row ID in \code{root.ChocolateBar} and \code{root.Contents}, respectively.}
    \li{Every row ID in \code{root.ChocolateBar} appears exactly once in column \code{a} of \code{root.unwrap}.}
    \li{The \code{root.charlies-bar} and \code{root.golden-ticket} tables each have exactly one row.}
    \li{Their values in column \code{a} are row IDs in \code{root.ChocolateBar} and \code{root.Contents}, respectively.}
    \li{The contents associated with Charlie's bar by \code{root.unwrap} must equal the value selected by \code{root.golden-ticket}.}
  }
}

\p{The field \code{plot-armour} becomes an equality constraint on the stored values, with no additional table.}

\p{More practically, we can use a dependent function to require an equation to hold for every input of some function, as in the theory of a bijection:}

\pre{%
\kw{theory} Bijection (X : \kwi{Set}) (Y : \kwi{Set}) := \kw{sig}
  fwd : X -> Y
  bwd : Y -> X
  bwd-fwd : (^c x : X) -> bwd (fwd x) = x
  fwd-bwd : (^c y : Y) -> fwd (bwd y) = y
\kw{end}
}

\p{\mvrnote{TODO} The \code{^c} annotations select conjunctive binders in Coln's mode system. We defer discussion of modes to [[001N]].}

\p{Another possible example of equality usage is asserting that a relation (in the sense of a function into \code{\kwi{Prop}}) is a partial function. For this, we require that if \code{x} is related to both \code{y0} and \code{y1}, then \code{y0} must be equal to \code{y1}.}

\pre{%
\kw{theory} IsPartialFunction (X : \kwi{Set}) (Y : \kwi{Set}) (R : X -> Y -> \kwi{Prop}) :=
  (x : X) -> (y0 y1 : Y) -> R x y0 -> R x y1 -> y0 = y1
}

\p{Equality is useful for constraints, but is especially useful for [queries](001A).}
